ions and obtain claim-specific independent review.", "Check exact theorem statement, assumptions and artifact dependencies before stronger acceptance."], "title": "Point convergence of Nesterov’s accelerated gradient method: an AI-assisted proof", "kind": "paper", "domain": "ai-mathematics"}</data></node><node id="ai-paper-mle"><data key="title">On Learning-Curve Monotonicity for Maximum Likelihood Estimators</data><data key="kind">paper</data><data key="domain">ai-mathematics</data><data key="status">selected-text-reviewed</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "ai-paper-mle", "sourceId": "ai-src-mle-paper", "topicIds": ["ai-mle", "ai-mle-bound"], "question": "When is a correctly specified MLE learning curve monotone?", "method": "Gaussian/Wishart and Gamma identities, complete monotonicity.", "result": "Exact finite-risk thresholds and monotonicity theorems, attributed to GPT5.2-assisted authors.", "whatIsNew": "Authors establish settings not previously covered; reverse-KL family links to old textbook insight.", "limitations": "Selected mathematical statements/assistance narrative; no formal build or peer-review certification.", "status": "selected-text-reviewed", "equationsUsed": ["ai-formula-kl"], "replication": "No original proof-system or benchmark rerun. Scalar identities and threshold counting are separately recomputed.", "nextQuestions": ["Pin source revisions and obtain claim-specific independent review.", "Check exact theorem statement, assumptions and artifact dependencies before stronger acceptance."], "title": "On Learning-Curve Monotonicity for Maximum Likelihood Estimators", "kind": "paper", "domain": "ai-mathematics"}</data></node><node id="ai-paper-batch3"><data key="title">First Proof Third Batch</data><data key="kind">paper</data><data key="domain">ai-mathematics</data><data key="status">selected-text-reviewed</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "ai-paper-batch3", "sourceId": "ai-src-third-pdf", "topicIds": ["ai-batch3", "ai-claude", "ai-openai-batch"],