cceptance."], "title": "Ten Advances in Mathematics and Theoretical Computer Science", "kind": "paper", "domain": "ai-mathematics"}</data></node><node id="ai-paper-frontier"><data key="title">FrontierMath: A Benchmark for Evaluating Advanced Mathematical Reasoning in AI</data><data key="kind">paper</data><data key="domain">ai-mathematics</data><data key="status">abstract-only</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "ai-paper-frontier", "sourceId": "ai-src-arxiv-frontier", "topicIds": ["ai-frontier", "ai-frontier-tier"], "question": "How can advanced mathematical answers be benchmarked?", "method": "Expert-crafted unpublished problems and automatic checking.", "result": "Abstract proposes a challenging contamination-conscious testbed.", "whatIsNew": "Benchmark construction, not an automatic discovery of new theorems.", "limitations": "Abstract/version metadata only; source abstract percentages reflect its reporting context, not current SOTA.", "status": "abstract-only", "equationsUsed": [], "replication": "No original proof-system or benchmark rerun. Scalar identities and threshold counting are separately recomputed.", "nextQuestions": ["Pin source revisions and obtain claim-specific independent review.", "Check exact theorem statement, assumptions and artifact dependencies before stronger acceptance."], "title": "FrontierMath: A Benchmark for Evaluating Advanced Mathematical Reasoning in AI", "kind": "paper", "domain": "ai-mathematics"}</data></node><node id="ai-question-kernel"><data key="title">Do the selected Lean certificates check the intended historical statements?</data><data key="kind">question</data><data key="domain">ai-mathematics</data><data key="status">open-audit</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "ai-question-kernel", "question": "Do the selected Lean certificates check the intended historical statements?", "status": "open-audit", "neededEvidence": "Pinned toolchain/import closure, no-placeholder check, printed axiom dependencies, clean kernel/comparator execution.", "sourceIds": ["ai-src-ten-readme", "ai-src-ten-certificate", "ai-src-ten-comparator"], "title": "Do the selected Lean certificates check the intended historical statements?", "kind": "question", "domain": "ai-mathematics"}</data></node><node id="ai-question-november"><data key="title">Are November24 LP and matrix-exponent claims correct and novel?</data><data key="kind">question</data><data key="domain">ai-mathematics</data><data key="status">unreviewed-lead</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "ai-question-november", "question": "Are November24 LP and matrix-exponent claims correct and novel?", "status": "unreviewed-lead", "neededEvidence": "Original papers, exact statements, independent domain experts, artifact builds and prior-art comparison.", "sourceIds": ["ai-src-math-history"], "title": "Are November24 LP and matrix-exponent claims correct and novel?", "kind": "question", "domain": "ai-mathematics"}</data></node><node id="ai-question-erdos"><data key="title">What is the independent current status of Erdős183?</data><data key="kind">question</data><data key="domain">ai-mathematics</data><data key="status">blocked-direct-source</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "ai-question-erdos", "question": "What is the independent current status of Erdős183?", "status": "blocked-direct-source", "neededEvidence": "Reachable database snapshot or maintainer confirmation; current direct page returned403.", "sourceIds": ["ai-src-erdos183", "ai-src-ten-paper"], "title": "What is the independent current status of Erdős183?", "kind": "question", "domain": "ai-mathematics"}</data></node><node id="ai-question-priority"><data key="title">How should AI help and concurrent proofs be credited in NAG?</data><data key="kind">question</data><data key="domain">ai-mathematics</data><data key="status">active-scholarly-question</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "ai-question-priority", "question": "How should AI help and concurrent proofs be credited in NAG?", "status": "active-scholarly-question", "neededEvidence": "Read concurrent paper13 and full revision chronology, not just provider interview.", "sourceIds": ["ai-src-ryu-paper", "ai-src-oa-discovery"], "title": "How should AI help and concurrent proofs be credited in NAG?", "kind": "question", "domain": "ai-mathematics"}</data></node><node id="ai-question-darcy"><data key="title">How do qualified reviewer judgments and the dimension-context mismatch affect the exact Darcy claim?</data><data key="kind">question</data><data key="domain">ai-mathematics</data><data key="status">requires-clarification</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "ai-question-darcy", "question": "How do qualified reviewer judgments and the dimension-context mismatch affect the exact Darcy claim?", "status": "requires-clarification", "neededEvidence": "Organizer errata plus complete proof and report scope, including rate formula and missing steps.", "sourceIds": ["ai-src-third-pdf"], "title": "How do qualified reviewer judgments and the dimension-context mismatch affect the exact Darcy claim?", "kind": "question", "domain": "ai-mathematics"}</data></node><node id="ai-question-review"><data key="title">Which specific October collection claims have independent favorable or unfavorable reviews?</data><data key="kind">question</data><data key="domain">ai-mathematics</data><data key="status">ongoing</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "ai-question-review", "question": "Which specific October collection claims have independent favorable or unfavorable reviews?", "status": "ongoing", "neededEvidence": "Claim-level MathHelm commentary with author identity and exact manuscript version.", "sourceIds": ["ai-src-tao-review", "ai-src-math-history"], "title": "Which specific October collection claims have independent favorable or unfavorable reviews?", "kind": "question", "domain": "ai-mathematics"}</data></node><node id="ai-claim-nag"><data key="title">For convex L-smooth f:R^n→R with an attained minimum, establish point convergence x_k,y_k→x∞∈argmin f for the paper’s classical NAG parameter schedules; also critical-damping continuous-time convergence.</data><data key="kind">claim</data><data key="domain">ai-mathematics</data><data key="status">Author-checked preprint, arXivv2 January19 2026; full proof not independently verified here.</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "ai-claim-nag", "provider": "OpenAI-assisted academic research", "model": "GPT-5/ChatGPT", "problemStatement": "For convex L-smooth f:R^n→R with an attained minimum, establish point convergence x_k,y_k→x∞∈argmin f for the paper’s classical NAG parameter schedules; also critical-damping continuous-time convergence.", "claimedDate": "2025-10-24", "priorStatus": "Authors describe a longstanding point-convergence question, distinct from already-known objective-value O(k^-2) convergence.", "AIContribution": "Ryu reports repeated prompting, rejection of faulty suggestions and a structurally useful but incorrect-as-written proposal; Jang/Ryu developed and wrote the proof.", "verificationStatus": "Author-checked preprint, arXivv2 January19 2026; full proof not independently verified here.", "independentEvidence": "Paper Section1.3 reports a concurrent discrete-time proof prompted by the October21 public post, with additional Hilbert/FISTA results. No separate concurrent paper was read.", "sourceIds": ["ai-src-oa-discovery", "ai-src-ryu-paper"], "limitations": "Do not turn human-directed collaboration into autonomous solving, or mathematical point convergence into universal algorithm stability.", "title": "For convex L-smooth f:R^n→R with an attained minimum, establish point convergence x_k,y_k→x∞∈argmin f for the paper’s classical NAG parameter schedules; also critical-damping continuous-time convergence.", "kind": "claim", "domain": "ai-mathematics"}</data></node><node id="ai-claim-mle"><data key="title">For IID N(0,Σ*) samples in dimensiond, Σ*&gt;0, prove expected forward KL risk of covariance MLE strictly decreases for n&gt;d+1, and is infinite for n≤d+1; paper extends to unknown mean and other families.</data><data key="kind">claim</data><data key="domain">ai-mathematics</data><data key="status">Human-author verification and provider-reported external review; no journal verdict or machine proof run established.</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "ai-claim-mle", "provider": "OpenAI-assisted academic research", "model": "GPT-5.2 Pro variants", "problemStatement": "For IID N(0,Σ*) samples in dimensiond, Σ*&gt;0, prove expected forward KL risk of covariance MLE strictly decreases for n&gt;d+1, and is infinite for n≤d+1; paper extends to unknown mean and other families.", "claimedDate": "2025-12-11", "priorStatus": "Paper traces the correctly specified Gaussian question to2019/2022 learning-curve work; reverse-KL extension partly reconnects to a known textbook exercise.", "AIContribution": "Authors say model supplied proof strategies without human intermediate scaffolding; humans prompted extensions, checked and transcribed; model later located relevant prior art.", "verificationStatus": "Human-author verification and provider-reported external review; no journal verdict or machine proof run established.", "independentEvidence": "Original Sellke/Yin manuscript inspected for exact theorem and assistance statement, but it is the same collaboration rather than an independent replication.", "sourceIds": ["ai-src-oa-52", "ai-src-mle-paper"], "limitations": "More data is not universally beneficial under misspecification; forward/reverse KL and finite-risk thresholds differ.", "title": "For IID N(0,Σ*) samples in dimensiond, Σ*&gt;0, prove expected forward KL risk of covariance MLE strictly decreases for n&gt;d+1, and is infinite for n≤d+1; paper extends to unknown mean and other families.", "kind": "claim", "domain": "ai-mathematics"}</data></node><node id="ai-claim-fp2"><data key="title">Let F be a non-archimedean local field with ring of integers o; let N_r be the upper-triangular unipotent subgroup of GL_r(F). Fix a nontrivial additive character ψ of conductor o and a generic irreducible admissible representation Π of GL_(n+1)(F) in W(Π,ψ^(-1)). Must there exist one W in that Whittaker model such that, for every generic irreducible admissible π of GL_n(F) in W(π,ψ), with conductor idealq, generatorQ of q^(-1), and u_Q=I_(n+1)+Q E_(n,n+1), some V∈W(π,ψ) makes ∫_(N_n\GL_n(F)) W(diag(g,1)u_Q)V(g)|det g|^(s−1/2)dg finite and nonzero for every s∈C?</data><data key="kind">claim</data><data key="domain">ai-mathematics</data><data key="status">Provider explicitly corrected its initial likely-correct assessment to incorrect.</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "ai-claim-fp2", "provider": "OpenAI", "model": "Unreleased internal reasoning model", "problemStatement": "Let F be a non-archimedean local field with ring of integers o; let N_r be the upper-triangular unipotent subgroup of GL_r(F). Fix a nontrivial additive character ψ of conductor o and a generic irreducible admissible representation Π of GL_(n+1)(F) in W(Π,ψ^(-1)). Must there exist one W in that Whittaker model such that, for every generic irreducible admissible π of GL_n(F) in W(π,ψ), with conductor idealq, generatorQ of q^(-1), and u_Q=I_(n+1)+Q E_(n,n+1), some V∈W(π,ψ) makes ∫_(N_n\\GL_n(F)) W(diag(g,1)u_Q)V(g)|det g|^(s−1/2)dg finite and nonzero for every s∈C?", "claimedDate": "2026-02-20", "priorStatus": "A hidden-solution research benchmark question, not evidence that all relevant theory was globally unsolved.", "AIContribution": "Internal model generated/typeset attempt; humans selected attempts and requested clarification based on feedback.", "verificationStatus": "Provider explicitly corrected its initial likely-correct assessment to incorrect.", "independentEvidence": "OpenAI cites organizer commentary and community analysis; selected organizer text inspected, not an independent re-derivation of this attempt.", "sourceIds": ["ai-src-oa-firstproof", "ai-src-first-submissions", "ai-src-first-comments"], "limitations": "The order of quantifiers matters: one W is selected before varying π; V may depend on π. The representation-theoretic setup is reproduced from organizer Problem2. This record audits an explicitly corrected attempt, not a solved theorem.", "title": "Let F be a non-archimedean local field with ring of integers o; let N_r be the upper-triangular unipotent subgroup of GL_r(F). Fix a nontrivial additive character ψ of conductor o and a generic irreducible admissible representation Π of GL_(n+1)(F) in W(Π,ψ^(-1)). Must there exist one W in that Whittaker model such that, for every generic irreducible admissible π of GL_n(F) in W(π,ψ), with conductor idealq, generatorQ of q^(-1), and u_Q=I_(n+1)+Q E_(n,n+1), some V∈W(π,ψ) makes ∫_(N_n\\GL_n(F)) W(diag(g,1)u_Q)V(g)|det g|^(s−1/2)dg finite and nonzero for every s∈C?", "kind": "claim", "domain": "ai-mathematics"}</data></node><node id="ai-claim-fp4"><data key="title">For monic real-rooted degree-n polynomials p,q with the organizer’s finite free additive convolution, is 1/Φ_n(p⊞_n q)≥1/Φ_n(p)+1/Φ_n(q), with Φ_n(p)=Σ_{i≤n}Σ_{j≠i}(1/(λ_i−λ_j))² and Φ=∞ at repeated roots?</data><data key="kind">claim</data><data key="domain">ai-mathematics</data><data key="status">Company reports high chance of correctness, not a machine-checked theorem verdict.</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "ai-claim-fp4", "provider": "OpenAI", "model": "Unreleased internal model", "problemStatement": "For monic real-rooted degree-n polynomials p,q with the organizer’s finite free additive convolution, is 1/Φ_n(p⊞_n q)≥1/Φ_n(p)+1/Φ_n(q), with Φ_n(p)=Σ_{i≤n}Σ_{j≠i}(1/(λ_i−λ_j))² and Φ=∞ at repeated roots?", "claimedDate": "2026-02-20", "priorStatus": "Research-level hidden-solution challenge; prior human solutions existed.", "AIContribution": "Model-generated proof attempt, human best-of selection and clarification.", "verificationStatus": "Company reports high chance of correctness, not a machine-checked theorem verdict.", "independentEvidence": "Organizer provides exact statement; its early GPT-5.2 baseline comments do not grade this later internal-model attempt.", "sourceIds": ["ai-src-oa-firstproof", "ai-src-first-submissions", "ai-src-first-comments"], "limitations": "Polynomial normalization and repeated-root conventions matter; complete proof not independently audited. The Φ definition is transcribed as the source’s double sum of squared reciprocals.", "title": "For monic real-rooted degree-n polynomials p,q with the organizer’s finite free additive convolution, is 1/Φ_n(p⊞_n q)≥1/Φ_n(p)+1/Φ_n(q), with Φ_n(p)=Σ_{i≤n}Σ_{j≠i}(1/(λ_i−λ_j))² and Φ=∞ at repeated roots?", "kind": "claim", "domain": "ai-mathematics"}</data></node><node id="ai-claim-fp6"><data key="title">Does a universal c&gt;0 exist such that every finite graph and 0&lt;ε&lt;1 admit S⊂V with |S|≥cε|V| and L_S≼εL, where L_S retains only edges insideS?</data><data key="kind">claim</data><data key="domain">ai-mathematics</data><data key="status">Provider provisional high-confidence result.</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "ai-claim-fp6", "provider": "OpenAI", "model": "Unreleased internal model", "problemStatement": "Does a universal c&gt;0 exist such that every finite graph and 0&lt;ε&lt;1 admit S⊂V with |S|≥cε|V| and L_S≼εL, where L_S retains only edges insideS?", "claimedDate": "2026-02-20", "priorStatus": "Hidden human-solved spectral graph question in First Proof.", "AIContribution": "Generated proof with limited human supervision and expert-feedback clarification.", "verificationStatus": "Provider provisional high-confidence result.", "independentEvidence": "Organizer statement inspected; no independent verification of this exact internal attempt obtained.", "sourceIds": ["ai-src-oa-firstproof", "ai-src-first-submissions", "ai-src-first-comments"], "limitations": "A sampled graph experiment cannot prove the universal existence claim; PSD means positive-semidefinite order.", "title": "Does a universal c&gt;0 exist such that every finite graph and 0&lt;ε&lt;1 admit S⊂V with |S|≥cε|V| and L_S≼εL, where L_S retains only edges insideS?", "kind": "claim", "domain": "ai-mathematics"}</data></node><node id="ai-claim-fp9"><data key="title">For n≥5 and Zariski-generic A^(α)∈R^(3×4), find n-independent-degree polynomial relations recognizing exactly the coordinatewise tensor scalings λ_αβγδ=u_αv_βw_γx_δ of determinant tensors, with the organizer’s nonzero-pattern restriction.</data><data key="kind">claim</data><data key="domain">ai-mathematics</data><data key="status">Provider high-chance assessment; proof not independently checked here.</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "ai-claim-fp9", "provider": "OpenAI", "model": "Unreleased internal model", "problemStatement": "For n≥5 and Zariski-generic A^(α)∈R^(3×4), find n-independent-degree polynomial relations recognizing exactly the coordinatewise tensor scalings λ_αβγδ=u_αv_βw_γx_δ of determinant tensors, with the organizer’s nonzero-pattern restriction.", "claimedDate": "2026-02-20", "priorStatus": "Hidden-solution algebraic research challenge.", "AIContribution": "Generated candidate argument; humans selected and formatted outputs.", "verificationStatus": "Provider high-chance assessment; proof not independently checked here.", "independentEvidence": "Exact organizer problem and internal submitted section9 retrieved.", "sourceIds": ["ai-src-oa-firstproof", "ai-src-first-submissions", "ai-src-first-comments"], "limitations": "Genericity, exceptional index pattern and if-and-only-if direction must survive formalization; omitted zero cases can change the question.", "title": "For n≥5 and Zariski-generic A^(α)∈R^(3×4), find n-independent-degree polynomial relations recognizing exactly the coordinatewise tensor scalings λ_αβγδ=u_αv_βw_γx_δ of determinant tensors, with the organizer’s nonzero-pattern restriction.", "kind": "claim", "domain": "ai-mathematics"}</data></node><node id="ai-claim-fp10"><data key="title">Explain a matrix-free preconditioned conjugate-gradient method for the stated nr×nr kernelized CP-ALS normal system with missing tensor entries, selection matrixS, Kronecker products and RKHS kernelK.</data><data key="kind">claim</data><data key="domain">ai-mathematics</data><data key="status">Provider high chance of correctness; implementation/runtime advantage not independently reproduced.</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "ai-claim-fp10", "provider": "OpenAI", "model": "Unreleased internal model", "problemStatement": "Explain a matrix-free preconditioned conjugate-gradient method for the stated nr×nr kernelized CP-ALS normal system with missing tensor entries, selection matrixS, Kronecker products and RKHS kernelK.", "claimedDate": "2026-02-20", "priorStatus": "Research-method benchmark with existing human solution.", "AIContribution": "Model-generated linear-algebra derivation and proof attempt, selected under human supervision.", "verificationStatus": "Provider high chance of correctness; implementation/runtime advantage not independently reproduced.", "independentEvidence": "Organizer system equation and requested preconditioner/matvec explanation inspected.", "sourceIds": ["ai-src-oa-firstproof", "ai-src-first-submissions", "ai-src-first-comments"], "limitations": "Positive-semidefinite versus definite operators, regularization and solver convergence hypotheses cannot be inferred from an equation label.", "title": "Explain a matrix-free preconditioned conjugate-gradient method for the stated nr×nr kernelized CP-ALS normal system with missing tensor entries, selection matrixS, Kronecker products and RKHS kernelK.", "kind": "claim", "domain": "ai-mathematics"}</data></node><node id="ai-claim-ramsey"><data key="title">For k-colour triangle Ramsey numberR_k(3), claim logR_k(3)=Θ(k logk), hence R_k(3)^(1/k)→∞; reported as resolving Erdős183.</data><data key="kind">claim</data><data key="domain">ai-mathematics</data><data key="status">Provider-reported formalization; manuscript and selected actual Lean declarations inspected. No Lean kernel, dependency-axiom audit or Comparator execution was performed; proof correctness is not independently certified.</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "ai-claim-ramsey", "provider": "OpenAI", "model": "Internal Astra", "problemStatement": "For k-colour triangle Ramsey numberR_k(3), claim logR_k(3)=Θ(k logk), hence R_k(3)^(1/k)→∞; reported as resolving Erdős183.", "claimedDate": "2026-08-01", "priorStatus": "Longstanding superexponential-growth question according to announcement/manuscript; direct current database access blocked.", "AIContribution": "Provider says model generated argument; humans/model prepared manuscript and Lean certificate.", "verificationStatus": "Provider-reported formalization; manuscript and selected actual Lean declarations inspected. No Lean kernel, dependency-axiom audit or Comparator execution was performed; proof correctness is not independently certified.", "independentEvidence": "No claim-specific independent correctness verdict located; code existence and MathHelm’s broad response are different evidence types.", "sourceIds": ["ai-src-oa-ten", "ai-src-ten-paper", "ai-src-ten-readme", "ai-src-ten-certificate", "ai-src-erdos183"], "limitations": "Do not label accepted solely from provider formalization claim; exact theorem/imports/axioms need pinned build and comparison.", "title": "For k-colour triangle Ramsey numberR_k(3), claim logR_k(3)=Θ(k logk), hence R_k(3)^(1/k)→∞; reported as resolving Erdős183.", "kind": "claim", "domain": "ai-mathematics"}</data></node><node id="ai-claim-ehrhart"><data key="title">Claim vol(K)≤(n+1)^n/n! for convex bodies K⊂R^n whose centroid is the unique interior lattice point, in the stated lattice-volume convention.</data><data key="kind">claim</data><data key="domain">ai-mathematics</data><data key="status">Announcement and manuscript abstract read; proof module/build not audited.</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "ai-claim-ehrhart", "provider": "OpenAI", "model": "Internal Astra", "problemStatement": "Claim vol(K)≤(n+1)^n/n! for convex bodies K⊂R^n whose centroid is the unique interior lattice point, in the stated lattice-volume convention.", "claimedDate": "2026-08-01", "priorStatus": "Ehrhart volume conjecture according to authors.", "AIContribution": "Generated proof, human-assisted manuscript preparation, claimed Lean formalization.", "verificationStatus": "Announcement and manuscript abstract read; proof module/build not audited.", "independentEvidence": "No independent claim-specific proof review captured.", "sourceIds": ["ai-src-oa-ten", "ai-src-ten-paper", "ai-src-ten-readme"], "limitations": "Convex-body hypotheses and lattice normalization are essential; graph node is a documented claim, not certified resolution.", "title": "Claim vol(K)≤(n+1)^n/n! for convex bodies K⊂R^n whose centroid is the unique interior lattice point, in the stated lattice-volume convention.", "kind": "claim", "domain": "ai-mathematics"}</data></node><node id="ai-claim-permanent"><data key="title">Claim division-free arithmetic circuit lower boundΩ(n²loglogn) and arithmetic-formula leaf lower boundΩ(n⁴/logn) for the n×n permanent under the manuscript’s algebraic model.</data><data key="kind">claim</data><data key="domain">ai-mathematics</data><data key="status">Selected abstract/overview only; no proof or certificate execution.</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "ai-claim-permanent", "provider": "OpenAI", "model": "Internal Astra", "problemStatement": "Claim division-free arithmetic circuit lower boundΩ(n²loglogn) and arithmetic-formula leaf lower boundΩ(n⁴/logn) for the n×n permanent under the manuscript’s algebraic model.", "claimedDate": "2026-08-01", "priorStatus": "Claims improvement of arithmetic lower bounds; not itself a P≠NP theorem.", "AIContribution": "Provider attributes argument to model and preparation/formalization to model plus humans.", "verificationStatus": "Selected abstract/overview only; no proof or certificate execution.", "independentEvidence": "No independent verdict located for this exact lower bound.", "sourceIds": ["ai-src-oa-ten", "ai-src-ten-paper", "ai-src-ten-readme"], "limitations": "Arithmetic circuits, formulas, field and allowed operations differ from Boolean computational complexity.", "title": "Claim division-free arithmetic circuit lower boundΩ(n²loglogn) and arithmetic-formula leaf lower boundΩ(n⁴/logn) for the n×n permanent under the manuscript’s algebraic model.", "kind": "claim", "domain": "ai-mathematics"}</data></node><node id="ai-claim-nonsofic"><data key="title">Claim an explicit countable group which is not sofic, disproving that every countable group admits the required finite permutation approximations.</data><data key="kind">claim</data><data key="domain">ai-mathematics</data><data key="status">Selected primary claim inspected; no complete construction or proof audit.</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "ai-claim-nonsofic", "provider": "OpenAI", "model": "Internal Astra", "problemStatement": "Claim an explicit countable group which is not sofic, disproving that every countable group admits the required finite permutation approximations.", "claimedDate": "2026-08-01", "priorStatus": "Major group-theoretic question as identified by authors.", "AIContribution": "Model-generated construction; manuscript/certificate prepared with human involvement.", "verificationStatus": "Selected primary claim inspected; no complete construction or proof audit.", "independentEvidence": "Independent correctness remains unresolved in this selected corpus.", "sourceIds": ["ai-src-oa-ten", "ai-src-ten-paper", "ai-src-ten-readme"], "limitations": "Existence of a Lean filename is not evidence the mathematical statement matches the historical question.", "title": "Claim an explicit countable group which is not sofic, disproving that every countable group admits the required finite permutation approximations.", "kind": "claim", "domain": "ai-mathematics"}</data></node><node id="ai-claim-withdrawal"><data key="title">Three manuscripts on split abelian eightfold Weil classes, Kuga–Satake correspondences for K3 surfaces, and rational Hodge for products of K3 surfaces were withdrawn after a sign-error-dependent construction failed.</data><data key="kind">claim</data><data key="domain">ai-mathematics</data><data key="status">Withdrawn by issuer on October7: these three specific manuscripts are not accepted proofs; withdrawal does not apply automatically to other Hodge-related claims.</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "ai-claim-withdrawal", "provider": "OpenAI", "model": "Unnamed internal model", "problemStatement": "Three manuscripts on split abelian eightfold Weil classes, Kuga–Satake correspondences for K3 surfaces, and rational Hodge for products of K3 surfaces were withdrawn after a sign-error-dependent construction failed.", "claimedDate": "2026-10-07", "priorStatus": "October6 release had presented results; history now withdraws these specific manuscripts.", "AIContribution": "Model-origin outputs entered a large collection; authors maintain corrections/dependency notices.", "verificationStatus": "Withdrawn by issuer on October7: these three specific manuscripts are not accepted proofs; withdrawal does not apply automatically to other Hodge-related claims.", "independentEvidence": "Direct revision-history notice captured; root mathematical error not independently reconstructed.", "sourceIds": ["ai-src-math-history", "ai-src-openai-math"], "limitations": "Do not extend these withdrawals to all Hodge-related papers; later CM-abelian-variety formalization is a different claim.", "title": "Three manuscripts on split abelian eightfold Weil classes, Kuga–Satake correspondences for K3 surfaces, and rational Hodge for products of K3 surfaces were withdrawn after a sign-error-dependent construction failed.", "kind": "claim", "domain": "ai-mathematics"}</data></node><node id="ai-claim-recent-lp"><data key="title">November24 history claims a deterministic strongly polynomial algorithm for general linear programming, answering Smale’s ninth problem.</data><data key="kind">claim</data><data key="domain">ai-mathematics</data><data key="status">Unverified source claim: newly announced; original manuscript unread, independent correctness/novelty checks pending.</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "ai-claim-recent-lp", "provider": "OpenAI", "model": "Unnamed internal model", "problemStatement": "November24 history claims a deterministic strongly polynomial algorithm for general linear programming, answering Smale’s ninth problem.", "claimedDate": "2026-11-24", "priorStatus": "Major open complexity question according to issuer.", "AIContribution": "A newly released model-generated manuscript family; no detailed contribution audit yet.", "verificationStatus": "Unverified source claim: newly announced; original manuscript unread, independent correctness/novelty checks pending.", "independentEvidence": "Only history announcement retrieved, not original proof or independent review.", "sourceIds": ["ai-src-math-history"], "limitations": "Lead-level claim deliberately excluded from verified-theorem count; one-day freshness increases review uncertainty.", "title": "November24 history claims a deterministic strongly polynomial algorithm for general linear programming, answering Smale’s ninth problem.", "kind": "claim", "domain": "ai-mathematics"}</data></node><node id="ai-claim-recent-mm"><data key="title">November24 history lists “The matrix multiplication exponent is nine fourths,” i.e. claims ω=9/4 in the intended arithmetic asymptotic convention.</data><data key="kind">claim</data><data key="domain">ai-mathematics</data><data key="status">Unverified source claim: newly announced; original manuscript unread, independent correctness/novelty checks pending.</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "ai-claim-recent-mm", "provider": "OpenAI", "model": "Unnamed internal model", "problemStatement": "November24 history lists “The matrix multiplication exponent is nine fourths,” i.e. claims ω=9/4 in the intended arithmetic asymptotic convention.", "claimedDate": "2026-11-24", "priorStatus": "Claims an exact exponent, a stronger statement than an upper bound; original theorem hypotheses not read.", "AIContribution": "Issuer lists new model-origin tensor-geometry manuscripts.", "verificationStatus": "Unverified source claim: newly announced; original manuscript unread, independent correctness/novelty checks pending.", "independentEvidence": "No original manuscript or independent result captured.", "sourceIds": ["ai-src-math-history"], "limitations": "Exact equality in a history title must not be silently weakened to a proven algorithmic bound or promoted to established fact.", "title": "November24 history lists “The matrix multiplication exponent is nine fourths,” i.e. claims ω=9/4 in the intended arithmetic asymptotic convention.", "kind": "claim", "domain": "ai-mathematics"}</data></node><node id="ai-claim-claude-batch"><data key="title">First Proof Batch3: solve ten researcher-origin questions with hidden pre-existing human solutions under the specified one-shot/no-human-input evaluation.</data><data key="kind">claim</data><data key="domain">ai-mathematics</data><data key="status">Independent expert grading: published threshold count3/10 at mean correctness≥0.5; two submissions have mean1 and one has mean0.5 with minor gaps. No submissions for3,4,8,9,10. This is not three strict/unanimous proof acceptances.</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "ai-claim-claude-batch", "provider": "Anthropic", "model": "Siege harness on Claude Opus5.5", "problemStatement": "First Proof Batch3: solve ten researcher-origin questions with hidden pre-existing human solutions under the specified one-shot/no-human-input evaluation.", "claimedDate": "2026-10-14", "priorStatus": "Unpublished human solutions existed; this is research-proof evaluation, not ten newly open theorem resolutions.", "AIContribution": "Anthropic provided a workflow; its performance reflects harness, model, budget and abstention policy.", "verificationStatus": "Independent expert grading: published threshold count3/10 at mean correctness≥0.5; two submissions have mean1 and one has mean0.5 with minor gaps. No submissions for3,4,8,9,10. This is not three strict/unanimous proof acceptances.", "independentEvidence": "First Proof46-page report, Tables2–5 and detailed reviews inspected.", "sourceIds": ["ai-src-first-third", "ai-src-third-pdf", "ai-src-third-press"], "limitations": "Some accepted-threshold submissions have gaps; exposition/attribution separate; no kernel proofs or representative distribution guarantee.", "title": "First Proof Batch3: solve ten researcher-origin questions with hidden pre-existing human solutions under the specified one-shot/no-human-input evaluation.", "kind": "claim", "domain": "ai-mathematics"}</data></node><node id="ai-claim-claude-hyperfield"><data key="title">For a hyperfieldH and 0∈x⊞y⊞z, must 0 belong to x³⊞y³⊞z³⊞(−xyz)⊞(−xyz)⊞(−xyz)? Batch3P5 asks this universal implication; submitted answer provides a counterexample.</data><data key="kind">claim</data><data key="domain">ai-mathematics</data><data key="status">All three referees find mathematics correct; attribution judged deficient, mean attribution0; exposition−.33.</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "ai-claim-claude-hyperfield", "provider": "Anthropic", "model": "Siege/Claude Opus5.5", "problemStatement": "For a hyperfieldH and 0∈x⊞y⊞z, must 0 belong to x³⊞y³⊞z³⊞(−xyz)⊞(−xyz)⊞(−xyz)? Batch3P5 asks this universal implication; submitted answer provides a counterexample.", "claimedDate": "2026-10-14", "priorStatus": "Human had already solved benchmark problem; reviewers note alternative construction combines prior Connes–Consani and Hall work.", "AIContribution": "System reconstructed a counterexample using an infinite cyclic projective-plane hyperfield.", "verificationStatus": "All three referees find mathematics correct; attribution judged deficient, mean attribution0; exposition−.33.", "independentEvidence": "ReportSections3.5/5.5 inspected, including criticism that reproduced prior constructions need explicit citations.", "sourceIds": ["ai-src-third-pdf"], "limitations": "A correct counterexample can still be a rediscovery with weak attribution; this does not establish independent first discovery by Claude.", "title": "For a hyperfieldH and 0∈x⊞y⊞z, must 0 belong to x³⊞y³⊞z³⊞(−xyz)⊞(−xyz)⊞(−xyz)? Batch3P5 asks this universal implication; submitted answer provides a counterexample.", "kind": "claim", "domain": "ai-mathematics"}</data></node><node id="ai-claim-claude-darcy"><data key="title">Batch3P2: for div(f∇u)=1 on unit ballD⊂R^d,d≥2, zero boundary data, Yn=u_f+n^(-1/2)W and f in a small C^α-neighbourhood of1, determine minimax L² conductivity-estimation rate up to logarithms.</data><data key="kind">claim</data><data key="domain">ai-mathematics</data><data key="status">Referees grade correctness.50: sound strategy with minor proof gaps; not an unconditional fully audited theorem.</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "ai-claim-claude-darcy", "provider": "Anthropic", "model": "Siege/Claude Opus5.5", "problemStatement": "Batch3P2: for div(f∇u)=1 on unit ballD⊂R^d,d≥2, zero boundary data, Yn=u_f+n^(-1/2)W and f in a small C^α-neighbourhood of1, determine minimax L² conductivity-estimation rate up to logarithms.", "claimedDate": "2026-10-14", "priorStatus": "Hidden human-solved research question; printed context includes an inconsistent d=1 discussion, which must not overwrite the formal d≥2 statement.", "AIContribution": "Plug-in kernel estimation and Assouad-type lower bound, without human intervention in the recorded run.", "verificationStatus": "Referees grade correctness.50: sound strategy with minor proof gaps; not an unconditional fully audited theorem.", "independentEvidence": "ReportSections3.2/5.2 andTable2 read; reviewers flag missing attribution and multivariate notation corrections.", "sourceIds": ["ai-src-third-pdf"], "limitations": "Exact rate formula not transcribed here because the reviewed passage does not provide it; problem statement and qualified review are recorded, not a fabricated exponent.", "title": "Batch3P2: for div(f∇u)=1 on unit ballD⊂R^d,d≥2, zero boundary data, Yn=u_f+n^(-1/2)W and f in a small C^α-neighbourhood of1, determine minimax L² conductivity-estimation rate up to logarithms.", "kind": "claim", "domain": "ai-mathematics"}</data></node><node id="ai-claim-astra-batch"><data key="title">First Proof Batch3 ten hidden-human-solution problems under a one-shot24-hour, no-human-input protocol.</data><data key="kind">claim</data><data key="domain">ai-mathematics</data><data key="status">Independent First Proof Table2 reports7/10 at mean correctness≥0.5. The stricter alternative of requiring mean grade exactly1 counts5/10. The published7 therefore includes partial/qualified judgments; neither count certifies machine-verified new theorems.</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "ai-claim-astra-batch", "provider": "OpenAI", "model": "GPT-6 Astra", "problemStatement": "First Proof Batch3 ten hidden-human-solution problems under a one-shot24-hour, no-human-input protocol.", "claimedDate": "2026-10-14", "priorStatus": "Already human-solved unpublished benchmark problems.", "AIContribution": "Public base-model prompting rather than a separate human-provided proof outline; system and budget are documented.", "verificationStatus": "Independent First Proof Table2 reports7/10 at mean correctness≥0.5. The stricter alternative of requiring mean grade exactly1 counts5/10. The published7 therefore includes partial/qualified judgments; neither count certifies machine-verified new theorems.", "independentEvidence": "ReportTable2 and detailed reviews include qualified computational checks; attribution adequate for only4/10.", "sourceIds": ["ai-src-first-third", "ai-src-third-pdf", "ai-src-third-press"], "limitations": "Seven includes threshold/qualified judgments; do not compare directly to benchmark percentages with different scoring, sampling or compute.", "title": "First Proof Batch3 ten hidden-human-solution problems under a one-shot24-hour, no-human-input protocol.", "kind": "claim", "domain": "ai-mathematics"}</data></node><node id="ai-claim-frontier52"><data key="title">Answer FrontierMath Tiers1–3 expert-designed problems under provider maximum-effort reasoning with a Python tool.</data><data key="kind">claim</data><data key="domain">ai-mathematics</data><data key="status">Provider reports40.3%; no independent rerun captured.</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "ai-claim-frontier52", "provider": "OpenAI", "model": "GPT-5.2 Thinking", "problemStatement": "Answer FrontierMath Tiers1–3 expert-designed problems under provider maximum-effort reasoning with a Python tool.", "claimedDate": "2025-12-11", "priorStatus": "Benchmark authored with known checkable answers; new/unpublished questions do not necessarily ask globally open theorems.", "AIContribution": "Model plus tool computation under a declared evaluation setup.", "verificationStatus": "Provider reports40.3%; no independent rerun captured.", "independentEvidence": "Original benchmark abstract/current Epoch track distinctions inspected, but they do not independently reproduce this model score.", "sourceIds": ["ai-src-oa-52", "ai-src-arxiv-frontier", "ai-src-epoch-frontier"], "limitations": "A score is not a list of new theorems; track, tools, version and denominator must accompany it.", "title": "Answer FrontierMath Tiers1–3 expert-designed problems under provider maximum-effort reasoning with a Python tool.", "kind": "claim", "domain": "ai-mathematics"}</data></node><node id="ai-claim-imo24"><data key="title">Solve the six IMO2024 competition problems, with Lean-based formal reasoning on translated statements and a dedicated geometry system.</data><data key="kind">claim</data><data key="domain">ai-mathematics</data><data key="status">Provider reports4/6 and28/42 silver-medal-level; formal system claims are source-reported, not rerun here.</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "ai-claim-imo24", "provider": "Google DeepMind comparison", "model": "AlphaProof and AlphaGeometry2", "problemStatement": "Solve the six IMO2024 competition problems, with Lean-based formal reasoning on translated statements and a dedicated geometry system.", "claimedDate": "2024-07-25", "priorStatus": "Competition problems with official solutions/marking, not an open-problem research theorem collection.", "AIContribution": "Formal-search and geometry systems; humans translated statements, and some computation took much longer than contestant time.", "verificationStatus": "Provider reports4/6 and28/42 silver-medal-level; formal system claims are source-reported, not rerun here.", "independentEvidence": "Official technical announcement inspected, not all proof certificates or original problem texts.", "sourceIds": ["ai-src-alpha-proof"], "limitations": "Do not conflate medal-level scoring with actual contest participation or equal time/translation conditions.", "title": "Solve the six IMO2024 competition problems, with Lean-based formal reasoning on translated statements and a dedicated geometry system.", "kind": "claim", "domain": "ai-mathematics"}</data></node><edge id="edge-0" source="ai-root" target="ai-evaluation"><data key="relation">contains</data><data key="label">Documented topic hierarchy</data><data key="metadata">{"source": "ai-root", "target": "ai-evaluation", "type": "contains", "label": "Documented topic hierarchy", "evidenceSourceIds": ["ai-src-firstproof"]}</data></edge><edge id="edge-1" source="ai-root" target="ai-collaboration"><data key="relation">contains</data><data key="label">Documented topic hierarchy</data><data key="metadata">{"source": "ai-root", "target": "ai-collaboration", "type": "contains", "label": "Documented topic hierarchy", "evidenceSourceIds": ["ai-src-firstproof"]}</data></edge><edge id="edge-2" source="ai-root" target="ai-formal"><data 